  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "$$\nx(x+3){y}^{\u2033}+(x-2){y}^{\u2032}+4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$",
  "worked_solution": "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+P(x){y}^{\u2032}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x+3){y}^{\u2033}+(x-2){y}^{\u2032}+4y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{\u2033}+\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}{y}^{\u2032}+\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-2}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{4}{x(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=0,$ since both denominators at $x=0$ equal zero. So, $x=0$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
